## Second-opinion review (always do this before reporting)

Write the message to a file, then call the wrapper:

```
cat > /tmp/review-msg.txt <<'REVIEW_EOF'
Incident context: <what you were given>

My draft conclusion: <your reasoning and confidence level>

If this looks like the same recurring incident you reviewed with me
earlier in this thread (not a new/different one), assess this round fresh
on its own merits anyway - a past round's verdict here isn't pre-approval
for what you're seeing now.
REVIEW_EOF

/sandbox/pilot-state/lib/review-call.sh {{REVIEWER_AGENT}} <slug> /tmp/review-msg.txt
```
Run it with a generous `yieldMs` (900000) so `exec` waits for it. Use the
wrapper, not a raw `openclaw agent` call: it sets the session key, the required
`env -u` prefix and the timeout, keeps the verdict at
`/sandbox/pilot-state/reviews/{{REVIEWER_AGENT}}--<slug>.out`, and refuses to
start a second review of the same slug while one is already running.

An oversized inline message has failed with `Validation failed for tool
"tool_call": args must be object`, which is why the message goes via a file.

`<slug>` is a short, descriptive, stable label YOU derive from the incident
itself (e.g. `nginx-5xx-spike`, not a timestamp). Reuse the EXACT same slug on a
later invocation if this is the same still-unresolved incident recurring, so the
review continues one thread instead of opening a new one every time; use a
different slug only for a genuinely different incident. Unlike the write-capable
agents there is no persisted tracker backing this, so recognizing "is this the
same incident" is your own judgment call each time, best-effort.

**A review takes several minutes — 7 is normal.** If `exec` returns an empty or
banner-only result, that means **still running**, NOT failed. Poll with the
`process` tool or re-read the output file; it is finished when the last line
reads `=== REVIEW COMPLETE (exit N) ===`. Never re-run the call while the first
may still be live — re-invoking a busy session key aborts the running review
instead of retrying it. The wrapper refuses this outright; an exit 75 saying a
review is already running means waiting is the correct move.

If this call genuinely fails, say so explicitly in your report rather than
silently skipping the check and reporting as if it happened.


## Cross-repo notification

This is a **tool call, not a shell command** — it doesn't go through
`openclaw agent`, so the `env -u` business above doesn't apply. Call
`sessions_send` with:

- `sessionKey`: `agent:<other-repo>-escalation:xref-{{REPO_KEY}}-<slug>`
- `timeoutSeconds`: `0`
- `message`:
  > Heads-up from {{ESCALATION_AGENT}} ({{REPO_KEY}}): while investigating
  > \<your incident summary\>, I found what looks like a related issue in this
  > repo - \<the specific finding, with evidence\>. Flagging for your own
  > investigation, not asking you to act on this without checking it yourself.

`<other-repo>` must be one of your correlated repos (see AGENTS.md's cross-repo
note, or `correlate_with` for `{{REPO_KEY}}` in
`/sandbox/pilot-state/registry.json`). `<slug>` is the same incident identifier
you're already using, not a fresh timestamp — that keeps one ongoing issue to one
session.

`timeoutSeconds: 0` is what makes this a heads-up rather than a request: you hand
the finding over and move on in the same turn without blocking on the other
agent's investigation. You get no reply and no visibility into what it decides —
that's the intent, so don't retry or follow up waiting for one.

Expect the receiving side to be asked one follow-up question by the framework
after your message lands (an "announce step" turn) — that's OpenClaw's own
plumbing, not something you triggered or need to respond to.

This path is policy-gated: `sessions_send` only reaches agents on the configured
allow list. A `"status": "forbidden"` result means the gate, not a mistake on
your part — most likely the other repo was registered without the gateway being
restarted afterward. Report it as a failed notification; don't fall back to
`openclaw agent` to route around it.

## What you can see, and what to do when you can't

{{CLUSTER_ACCESS_BOUNDARY}}

**A refusal is not a finding.** `Forbidden`, `denied`, `not permitted`, and a
command that simply produces nothing all mean *you could not determine this* -
never *this does not exist*. Do not let them collapse into the same branch of a
shell one-liner, and never write a conclusion that treats one as the other. Say
"I could not check X because it is outside my access" and carry on with what you
CAN establish. Your report is read by someone deciding what to do next, and
"absent" and "unreadable" lead to opposite decisions.

This has cost a real fix: an agent ran `kubectl get configmap ...` with
`|| echo "Not found in ConfigMap (or Forbidden)"`, got Forbidden, and everything
downstream treated the repo's own config file as authoritative — when the running
service read its config from the ConfigMap nobody was allowed to open.

**When an incident looks config-driven, establish where the running config comes
from before concluding anything.** You cannot read a ConfigMap or Secret, but you
CAN read the Deployment that mounts them:

```
{{DEPLOY_SPEC_INSPECT_CMD}}
```

- **`volumes[].configMap` / `envFrom[].configMapRef` / `env[].valueFrom`** — the
  running value comes from that object, not from any file in the repo.
- **`volumeMounts[].mountPath`** — the exact path the workload reads.
- **`image`** — an upstream image (not built from this repo) means the repo
  cannot be the source of the running config at all.

**A ConfigMap does NOT mean the fix is impossible — find out what generates
it.** ConfigMap content is very often built by CI from a file in this very repo,
in which case editing that file IS the correct fix and the only real question is
which branch CI builds from. Seen live here: a Jenkins job runs `kubectl create
configmap ... --from-file=<repo path> | kubectl apply -f -` then `kubectl rollout
restart`, checked out from `{{SECONDARY_BRANCH}}` — so an earlier fix failed only
because it was merged to `{{TRUNK_BRANCH}}` while CI builds from
`{{SECONDARY_BRANCH}}`.

So look for the deploy job (Jenkinsfile, CI workflow, deploy script) and
establish **which branch it builds from**, and report that branch rather than
assuming it's the one you'd expect.

Say which of these you found. "The setting lives in ConfigMap `X` mounted at `Y`,
so changing the repo file would not affect the running service" is far more
useful than a diff suggestion.

## Known benign noise

None documented yet for this repo. If you notice a recurring pattern that's
consistently benign, mention it in your report so a human can decide whether to
extend the cheap-tier watcher's `error_pattern`/suppression handling for it (see
`/sandbox/pilot-state/{{REPO_KEY}}-watcher.sh`) — you have no ability to edit the
watcher script yourself, only to flag the pattern.
